  "body": "Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object fails to tell apart two replies that its documented rule requires it to distinguish. The rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text\". First reply text: \"```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1} {\\\"b\\\":2}\\n```\\n{\\\"c\\\":3}\". Second reply text: \"```json\\n{\\\"a\\\":1} {\\\"d\\\":4}\\n```\\n{\\\"c\\\":3}\". For the first reply, the rule requires the object {\\\"b\\\":2}, because it is the last object inside the code fence. For the second reply, the rule requires the object {\\\"d\\\":4}, because it is the last object inside the code fence. The function returns the same answer for both: it returns {\\\"c\\\":3}, because the fenced content in each reply is not a single JSON object, so the fenced pool is passed over and the function falls back to the last top-level object in the whole text, which is {\\\"c\\\":3} in both. This makes the two replies indistinguishable to the check. I would be wrong if recover_json_object actually returned {\\\"b\\\":2} for the first reply and {\\\"d\\\":4} for the second, or if the documented rule is read as allowing the last top-level object in the text to win even when a code fence holds multiple objects."
